WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.340
Dynamic invocation is security-sensitive because it turns runtime names and metadata into behavior.

2
00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:17.480
A string, attribute value, configuration entry, or file record can influence which constructor, method, property, event, or handler is used.

3
00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.260
That is a powerful pattern, but it needs clear boundaries.

4
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:28.740
Avoid selecting types, constructors, methods, properties, or events directly from user-controlled strings.

5
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.200
A request value should not become a raw type name.

6
00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:37.260
A configuration setting should not grant access to any class on the probing path.

7
00:00:37.360 --> 00:00:41.040
A plugin identifier should not become unrestricted assembly discovery.

8
00:00:41.320 --> 00:00:49.720
Prefer explicit maps, allowlists, fixed contracts, command registries, dependency injection registrations, or typed interfaces.

9
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:53.660
These patterns keep the dynamic choice bounded.

10
00:00:53.760 --> 00:01:00.080
The application can expose stable operation keys without letting external data choose arbitrary runtime members.

11
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:04.840
Validate and authorize the requested operation before invoking reflected code.

12
00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:13.660
Treat constructor arguments, method arguments, property values, and attribute-derived values as untrusted unless they come from a trusted source.

13
00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:22.160
Runtime activation should not skip normal dependency lifetime, authorization, validation, logging, or error-handling expectations.

14
00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:25.480
Reflection exceptions should be handled safely.

15
00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:33.340
Failure responses should avoid exposing internal names, paths, stack details, or sensitive values unnecessarily.

16
00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:40.320
Logs should preserve enough safe context for troubleshooting without turning internal reflection behavior into public documentation.
